  "slug": "metabolomics-mechanism-6f28",
  "summary": "Across October 2026 publications, metabolomics is paired with network pharmacology and in vivo validation to link compounds to pathway-level targets.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread across these studies is the use of untargeted or targeted metabolomics, often combined with network pharmacology, transcriptomics, proteomics, or gut microbiota profiling, to nominate pathway-level mechanisms for botanical extracts, polysaccharides, and single compounds in rodent disease models. Examples include glutathione metabolism and ferroptosis suppression for SXZ in aged rat hippocampus, purine metabolism and xanthine dehydrogenase inhibition for Ginkgo biloba extract in hyperuricemic nephropathy, and arachidonic acid metabolism via PPARα-CYP4A14 for Ya-Jie-Sha-Ba Decoction in alcohol-associated liver disease. Several studies use machine learning or WGCNA to prioritize hub targets, such as TLR4 and Nrf2 for Acorus tatarinowii polysaccharides and CHEK2 and HPGD for berberine. The novelty lies less in any single pathway than in the convergence of metabolite-level readouts with target prioritization and experimental validation; the cerebral ischemia study extends this by proposing a flavopiridol-related metabolite as a biomarker (ROC AUC 0.94). The HBEGF, TMEM121, and nanoparticle studies instead address signaling and delivery.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-10-01"
}
